WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.003
The admin console lists payments newest first, 20 to a page.

2
00:00:07.003 --> 00:00:12.740
An index on created at and payment id keeps every payment in exactly that order.

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:15.912
Each page asks for LIMIT 20 and an OFFSET.

4
00:00:15.912 --> 00:00:21.373
Page 1 reads the newest 20 entries and stops, in a fraction of a millisecond.

5
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.059
Page 5,001 is OFFSET 100,000.

6
00:00:24.059 --> 00:00:30.419
An index stores no row numbers, so PostgreSQL can't jump to entry 100,001.

7
00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:33.341
It starts at the newest and walks.

8
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:39.331
It reads 100,020 rows, and throws away 100,000 of them to return 20.

9
00:00:39.331 --> 00:00:42.144
That takes about 50 milliseconds.

10
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:47.530
Page 45,001 reads 900,020 rows and takes 1.2 seconds.

11
00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.648
Every page costs more than the one before.

12
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:55.347
OFFSET also goes wrong while payments arrive.

13
00:00:55.347 --> 00:00:59.368
With five to a page, page 1 shows payments 37 to 33.

14
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.576
Then a new payment comes in at the front.

15
00:01:02.576 --> 00:01:08.668
Every row slides back one place, so page 2, OFFSET 5, starts with payment 33 again.

16
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:10.437
Fix both by not counting.

17
00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:15.247
The client already knows where the last page ended: the last row it showed.

18
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:23.397
Keyset pagination asks only for rows that sort after that key, comparing created at and payment id as a pair.

19
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:30.757
On page 5,001, PostgreSQL descends the index once, straight to the cursor, and reads the next 20.

20
00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:33.192
Under a millisecond, like page 1.

21
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:36.550
Payments that arrive later sort before the cursor.

22
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.969
They land on page 1 and shift nothing.

23
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:42.984
You give up page numbers: there's no jump to page 4,000.

24
00:01:42.984 --> 00:01:47.073
For an infinite scroll or an export job, that costs nothing.

25
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:51.040
OFFSET counts its way to the page, and reads every row it skips.

26
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.344
A cursor remembers where you stopped, and the index jumps straight there.
